% tcolorbox's `breakable` library (used by solution/proofbox/tosubmit below,
% for page-breaking bookkeeping) resets \parindent to 0 internally, so
% without restoring it, every paragraph after the first inside these boxes
% loses its first-line indent -- even though plain body text and \problem
% (not a tcolorbox) keep theirs. Capture the real \parindent once, so it
% always matches whatever the surrounding document actually uses.
%
% This deliberately does NOT also force indentation on each box's own FIRST
% paragraph (unlike every later one) -- LaTeX never auto-indents the first
% paragraph after a sectional unit/environment by default anyway (same
% reason plain body text's own very first paragraph isn't indented either),
% so this matches that existing convention rather than fighting it.
% Tried forcing it via \indent and separately via \leavevmode\hspace{...}
% appended to `before upper`; both intermittently broke the *second*
% paragraph's indentation instead (tcolorbox's `breakable` library
% apparently doesn't always close its own internal \parindent-zeroing group
% cleanly, so which paragraphs came out indented depended on unrelated
% content elsewhere in the document -- not reproducible by any single
% property of the box or its title). Not worth that risk for what's a
% cosmetic nicety; \setlength alone is the version actually verified stable.
\newlength{\cedtparindent}
\AtBeginDocument{\setlength{\cedtparindent}{\parindent}}

% Solution/proofbox/tosubmit below each take an optional tcolorbox keyval
% argument (their trailing #1) that's appended after every default they set,
% so any key -- most usefully title=... -- overrides that box's default for
% one use, e.g. \begin{solution}[title=Alternate Answer] ... \end{solution},
% with no need to touch this file. Same mechanism as codingbox's
% [language=C++]; see templates/style-guide/style-guide.tex for a worked
% example.

